{"name":"Suez Canal","hex":"#235e80","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Suez Canal","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A canal in Egypt, linking the Mediterranean Sea and the Red Sea."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Suez_Canal"}],"entries":[{"title":"Suez Canal","blocks":[{"text":"The Suez Canal is an artificial sea-level waterway in Egypt, connecting the Mediterranean Sea to the Red Sea through the Isthmus of Suez and dividing Africa and Asia. The 193.3-kilometre-long (120.1-mile) canal is a key trade route between Europe and Asia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Iss016e019375.jpg/330px-Iss016e019375.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suez_Canal"},{"title":"Suez","blocks":[{"text":"Suez is a seaport city in north-eastern Egypt, located on the north coast of the Gulf of Suez on the Red Sea, near the southern terminus of the Suez Canal. The city is the capital and largest city of the Suez Governorate. The modern city of Suez is a successor of the ancient Egyptian city of Clysma, a major Red Sea port and a center of monasticism."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/EG-suez-20-bg-suez.jpg/330px-EG-suez-20-bg-suez.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suez"},{"title":"Canal","blocks":[{"text":"Canals or artificial waterways are waterways or engineered channels built for drainage management or for conveyancing water transport vehicles. They carry free, calm surface flow under atmospheric pressure, and can be thought of as artificial rivers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Alter_Strom.jpg/330px-Alter_Strom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canal"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia